# Flow versus Saga

| Contract | Flow | Saga |
| --- | --- | --- |
| "'`apply` result | `Relux.Flow.Result` (ActionResult) | `Void` |
| Caller gets operation success/failure | Yes | Observe domain state/actions instead |
| Async work awaited | Yes | Yes; Void does not mean detached |
| State mutation | Dispatch actions to reducers | Dispatch actions to reducers |
| Current example | Notes operations and reusable Auth | Error tracking |

## Notes: return the operation outcome

[Notes.Business.Flow](../../relux_sample/Modules/Notes/Business/Middleware/Notes+Business+Flow.swift)
calls an injected service. Successful mutations fetch a canonical, revisioned
snapshot from the provider and dispatch it to the reducer. Stale editor metadata
cannot remove protection, and stale snapshots cannot restore a body after relock.
Failures return failure; concurrent error tracking never converts them to success.
The service rejects blank titles/content before writing to the provider.

[Create.Container](../../relux_sample/Modules/Notes/UI/Create/Notes+UI+Create+Container.swift)
awaits `actions(actions: { Notes.Business.Effect.upsert(note: note) })`; success
dismisses the editor and failure preserves its draft. Views invoke callbacks.
Awaiting reduction does not guarantee Combine delivery or SwiftUI rendering has run.

## Auth: return reusable authorization

[Auth.Business.Flow](../../Packages/Auth/Sources/AuthReluxImpl/Business/Middleware/Auth+Business+Flow.swift)
accepts only `.success(true)` from the service. False, cancellation, unavailability
and errors return failure. It has no navigation, global login state or UI package.
The typed `authenticate()` result allows callers outside Relux dispatch to await
it without inspecting logged actions. `apply` exposes the same behavior to Relux.

The composition root injects that operation into Notes. The Notes provider owns
which note a successful evaluation grants, and rejects late results after
revocation. See [orchestration](RELUX_ORCHESTRATION.md) and [the protection
contract](../../.spec/note-protection.md).

## Validate both sides

Assert results and resulting state, with false/failure/cancellation cases and
narrowing mutations. [Testing Strategy](TESTING_STRATEGY.md) lists executable
examples and separates deterministic unit coverage from native system UI bounds.
EOF
cat > Docs/Patterns/RELUX_ORCHESTRATION.md <<'"'EOF'
# Relux orchestration

Cross-domain coordination lives at the composition boundary. [App IoC](../../relux_sample/IoC/IoC.swift)
constructs "'`Auth.Module(service:)` and passes its typed `flow.authenticate()`
operation to [Notes.Module](../../relux_sample/Modules/Notes/Notes+Module.swift).
Notes does not import AuthServiceImpl or LocalAuthentication, and Auth does not
import Notes. A result-bearing Flow provides the outcome needed by the caller.

## Actual entry points

Notes containers dispatch obtain, upsert, delete, protect, unlock and relock
effects. The Notes Flow delegates through its service to the in-memory provider.
The provider owns protection metadata, transient per-note grants and generation
checks. Only successful system authentication for the requested note can grant
access. The Flow publishes canonical revisioned snapshots after mutations.

The root container revokes all grants on background, and removes presentation
content while that work runs. Inactive transitions during a system authentication
prompt do not revoke grants. Explicit relock uses the same provider boundary.
Neither path erases notes. See [note-protection.md](../../.spec/note-protection.md).

## Ordering and ownership

Module registration does not establish subscriber execution order. IoC registers
ErrorHandling, Navigation, Auth and Notes with one Store and RootSaga. Serial
dispatch orders actions within that dispatch, not independent saga subscribers.
Provider actor isolation, authentication generations and snapshot revisions enforce
the access invariant without assuming a subscriber order.

The old app-global login/logout orchestration is removed. The retained diagrams
are labeled historical in [their index](../../diagrams/README.md). A larger app
may introduce an app-local Saga to observe cross-domain events when no caller
needs a result. Extract it only when ownership or reuse warrants another module.
EOF
